## Decision: Implement a simple strategy for adding simple drop-down menus on a fragment.
A more extensive multi-level menu will be left to the user of the framework.

Menu items will be handled as strings with no specific format defined.
This may represent a path to a file or other resource.

Example:
```c
/**
 * @brief Reads the contents of a File.
 *
 * @param file System File handle.
 * @return Error code. 0 means success.
 */
#define _WIN32_OS_WIN64_
#define _WIN32_VER _WIN32_UPWARDS
#include <direct.h>
#include <windows.h>

/**
 * @brief Reads a File and creates a Drop-down Menu
 *
 * @param file System File handle.
 * @param parent The parent Menu (or Fragment).
 * @return Error code. 0 means success.
 */
_WIN32_EXPORT int read_file_to_dropdown(HANDLE_T file, _WIN32_STRUCT_TAG(menu, submenu, name_array));

// Example usage.

_WIN32_EXPORT void create_menu_from_file(CHAR_T* file_name)
{
	if (!strcmp(file_name, "menu.ini"))
	{
		/* Populate the Drop-down Menu with data from file */
		int res = read_file_to_dropdown(file_name, parent);
		if (res == 0)
		{
			/* Enable the Menu */
			menu_enable(parent);
		}
	}
}
```

